# Normalise line endings in the repository, and check out native ones.
#
# Without this, files created on Windows land in the repository with CRLF and
# every clone on another platform reports the whole file as modified. It also
# keeps diffs readable: a patch touching one line should show one line.
* text=auto

# Scripts that are executed must keep LF regardless of platform. A shell script
# with CRLF fails on anything but Windows, and the failure is a confusing
# "bad interpreter" rather than a line-ending complaint.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf

# The launcher and the SDL tree are built by clang. Sources there are fine with
# either ending, but pinning them keeps the tree consistent with what the
# compiler has already been tested against.
*.c       text eol=lf
*.h       text eol=lf

# Source and config WE author. Every text blob in this repository is LF, but
# `text=auto` only says how to NORMALISE on the way in -- it leaves the checkout
# side to core.autocrlf, which is `true` on this machine and therefore wants
# CRLF. The mismatch produced a phantom "modified" on module.json5 after a tool
# rewrote it with LF endings, with the content byte-identical to the blob. That
# cost a round of confusion; pinning the checkout side to what is actually
# stored removes the whole class of it.
#
# Measured before adding this: of every blob in the repository, only the 14 PNGs
# contain CRLF, and they are declared `binary` below. No text file is affected.
*.json5   text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.ets     text eol=lf
*.ts      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf

# Binary payloads. These are not committed (see .gitignore) but stating it here
# means a future `git add -f` cannot corrupt them.
*.so      binary
*.so.*    binary
*.hap     binary
*.jar     binary
*.p12     binary
*.p7b     binary
*.cer     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpeg    binary
